WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.970
There's never been another superhero movie
as epic as Avengers: Infinity War, so it's

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.220
time to look into everything that went into
crafting this blockbuster, from the sheer

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.320
scale of the star-studded production to the
improvised line or two.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.900
And stay tuned to find out which of your favorite
Marvel heroes survived the snap offscreen.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.820
The road to war

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.080
According to writers Christopher Markus and
Stephen McFeely, work on the Infinity War

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.160
script started around January 2016.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.850
The story went through several iterations
before arriving at what we saw, including

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.140
versions written well before Thor: Ragnarok
and Black Panther were completed.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.780
That means a lot of changes had to be made
to Infinity War, from small omissions to major

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.040
structural shifts.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.200
Howard the Duck originally made a brief re-appearance;
Thor bantered a little more with the Guardians

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.460
of the Galaxy; extra backstory for the Black
Order was included; and we even almost saw

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.280
a destructive sequence on Xandar in which
Thanos claims the Power Stone.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.680
But perhaps most intriguing of these changes
involved Steve Rogers, whose role in the final

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.480
cut was already pretty limited.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.440
Markus and McFeely note on Infinity War’s
commentary that, in one version of the film,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.040
Cap didn’t show up until the moment he saves
Vision from Corvus Glaive in Wakanda, well

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.240
into the final act.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.640
"And then after we read the draft, we did
conclude that we were all insane.”

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:27.680
An epic production

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.539
Infinity War’s story spans multiple planets
and several continents on Earth, features

00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:39.060
dozens of speaking roles and a range of massive
set-pieces which take us from Edinburgh to

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.380
Wakanda to Titan.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.980
It stands to reason that the production would
be nothing short of massive, too, and the

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.540
numbers don't disappoint.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.600
Consider that many of the crew who worked
on-site had to be paid, fed, and often even

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.200
housed, and you’ll get a good idea of how
big this film really was.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.340
According to co-director Joe Russo in the
featurette Beyond The Battle: Wakanda, over

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:07.120
6,000 people worked on the film at one point
or another, all collaborating to create over

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.240
3,000 shots.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:14.760
Of those shots, 2,900 required some form of
visual effects in post-production, which meant

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:19.860
that a portion of the Infinity War effects
team had to add something to just about every

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:20.980
second of the film.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:22.920
Influences

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.260
Obviously, the comics themselves had a major
influence on the plot of Infinity War, but

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.280
they weren’t alone.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.530
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo are, of course,
huge fans of cinema, and a number of different

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.400
films are referenced throughout Infinity War.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.410
On the film's commentary track, the moment
in which Ebony Maw is burned while attempting

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.880
to steal the Time Stone is a nod to Steven
Spielberg's Raiders of the Lost Ark.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:00.040
A later scene in which Maw tortures Strange
with needles is a reference to Marathon Man,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.680
while, during interviews around the film’s
release, the Russos also highlighted a number

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:10.570
of crime films as influences on Infinity War's
heist-like elements, including Out of Sight

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:11.940
and Two Days in the Valley.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.100
Room for improvisation

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.660
As massive as it is, Infinity War required
incredible amounts of planning and structuring

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.620
in order to properly execute the script on-screen.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.800
Despite that, however, the filmmakers still
found room for a little improvisation along

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.300
the way.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.660
“For the record this was my plan.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.880
It was a nice contribution by Mr. Pratt."

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.280
In Beyond the Battle: Wakanda, the filmmakers
note that the Black Panther cast were performing

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.900
the Wakandan war chants off camera as a way
to warm themselves up for the action.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.820
The Russos heard the chants and decided to
incorporate them into the film.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.640
Not all of the new additions came from the
cast, though.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:56.720
On the commentary track, the filmmakers note
that Rocket referring to the Outriders as

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.720
"space dogs" came from one viewer who called
the creatures that name during a focus group

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.120
discussion after an early test screening.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.600
They thought it was funny, and added it to
Bradley Cooper's dialogue for his Rocket recording

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.340
sessions.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.660
The living and the dead

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:18.380
Infinity War ends with one of the most devastating
gut punches in cinematic history: a montage

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:24.120
spanning two planets, showing us the deaths
of numerous Marvel favorites, including Spider-Man,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.540
Star-Lord, Scarlet Witch, Black Panther, and
eventually even Nick Fury.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:30.820
“Oh no.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:33.520
Motherf-...”

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.100
It feels like a huge loss, but this is only
a tiny portion of the devastation Thanos inflicted

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.110
with his snap.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.830
Remember, we're talking about half of all
life in the universe here, which means half

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.680
of anyone you've ever seen, loved or hated
in an MCU film or series.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.780
Naturally, the filmmakers also made sure to
keep track of which characters survived and

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:59.640
which disintegrated offscreen.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.720
On the "survived" side, you’ve got Howard
the Duck, Spider-Man's Aunt May, and Shuri

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.000
and Nakia from Wakanda.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.840
On the "snapped" side, there's Incredible
Hulk co-star Betty Ross, the Asgardian Lady

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.070
Sif, and, as we later learned thanks to Ant-Man
and the Wasp, Hank Pym, and Hope and Janet

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:18.900
van Dyne.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.740
Of course, it’s a safe bet that none of
them will be gone for too long.

